{"version" 2 "boxes" [{"id" "three-output-scene" "properties" {"children" #{} "parents" #{#afield/box "root"} "name" "1 — one scene, two kinds of thing" "frame" #afield/rect [60.0 60.0 480.0 100.0] "code" #afield/file-inline {"path" "three-output-scene_code_.kt" "inline" "/*\nONE SCENE, TWO KINDS OF THING.\n\nBefore `Box.three { }`, a solid and the lines that annotate it could not share a\nviewer: TriMeshes went to the CPU rasterizer and came back as a still picture, and\nFLines went to the three.js viewer. This box is the thing that was impossible.\n\nA gyroid marched inside a finite box is an OPEN surface -- it leaves the sampling\nwindow rather than closing inside it, and every place it does that is a boundary\nloop. A shaded surface hides exactly that: the rim reads as a silhouette.\n*/\n\nval surface = marchingCubes(sdfGyroid(4.0, 0.3), boundsAround(radius = 1.0), 72)\nprintln(surface.summary()) // ... chi=-20, genus 4, open(20)\n\n// The rim, as FLines, in the mesh's own coordinates. One closed ring per hole.\nval rims =\n    surface.toFLines(MeshLines.BOUNDARY).map { ring ->\n        ring.color = vec(1.0, 0.45, 0.12, 1.0)\n        ring.thickness = 2.6\n        ring\n    }\nprintln(\"open boundary loops: \" + rims.size)\n\nthree(channel = \"gyroid\", height = 470, width = 760) {\n    mesh(surface, wireframe = false)\n    lines(rims)\n    camera(yaw = 32.0, pitch = 20.0, distance = 2.05)\n    title(\"marched gyroid, with its \" + rims.size + \" open boundary loops drawn in the same scene\")\n    caption(\"the solid and the annotation are one scene in model space, so orbiting moves both\")\n}\n\n/*\nNow ORBIT it. The rings stay welded to the surface, because both are in model\nspace and there is one camera. The HUD's counts, top right, are the whole\nscene's -- the drawables' triangles are added to the FLine half rather than\nreplacing it.\n\nThings to try:\n  mesh(surface, wireframe = true)     // count the triangles\n  helpers(true)                       // the grid and tripod, off by default for a mesh\n  MeshLines.CREASES instead of BOUNDARY, for the feature lines a drawing would show\n*/\n"} "nested" #{}}} {"id" "three-output-grid" "properties" {"children" #{} "parents" #{#afield/box "root"} "name" "2 — a contact sheet: threeGrid" "frame" #afield/rect [60.0 200.0 480.0 100.0] "code" #afield/file-inline {"path" "three-output-grid_code_.kt" "inline" "/*\nA CONTACT SHEET IS N VIEWPORTS, NOT N VIEWERS.\n\nThe obvious way to draw four meshes side by side is four iframes, and it is wrong:\neach iframe is its own WebGL context, Chrome caps live contexts at around sixteen\nand silently evicts the oldest, and the evicted one comes back as a blank panel\nwith nothing in the console. So a ThreePanel is a VIEWPORT -- one context,\nsetScissorTest and a per-panel setViewport in the render loop.\n\nIn a GRID each panel frames its OWN bounds, so a small mesh is not lost beside a\nlarge one. (A turntable wants the opposite; see the next box.)\n*/\n\nval ball = marchingCubes(sdfSphere(1.0), boundsAround(radius = 1.3), 40)\nval drilled = marchingCubes(sdfSubtract(sdfSphere(1.0), sdfCylinder(0.34, 4.0)), boundsAround(radius = 1.3), 64)\nval twisted = marchingCubes(sdfBox(vec(1.2, 1.8, 0.4)).twisted(0.35), boundsAround(radius = 1.2), 72)\nval ring = torusMesh(64, 32, 0.62, 0.24)\n\nthreeGrid(\n    \"marched sphere, resolution 40\" to ball,\n    \"...drilled through: the drill added a handle\" to drilled,\n    \"sdfBox(...).twisted(0.35)\" to twisted,\n    \"torusMesh(64, 32) - genus 1 by construction\" to ring,\n    columns = 2,\n    facts = true, // <- OFF by default, and showMeshGrid does not turn it on\n    cellWidth = 430,\n    height = 760,\n    channel = \"grid\",\n    view = MeshView(pitchDegrees = 44.0),\n)\n\n/*\nThings to try:\n  facts = false               // the fact line under each panel disappears; this is\n                              // what `showMeshGrid(...)` gives you\n  columns = 4                 // one WebGL context re-tiles itself\n  view = MeshView(pitchDegrees = 12.0)   // the drilled bore leaves the frame\n*/\n"} "nested" #{}}} {"id" "three-output-turntable" "properties" {"children" #{} "parents" #{#afield/box "root"} "name" "3 — a turntable: one upload, four angles" "frame" #afield/rect [60.0 340.0 480.0 100.0] "code" #afield/file-inline {"path" "three-output-turntable_code_.kt" "inline" "/*\nA TURNTABLE IS THE OPPOSITE OF A GRID, IN TWO WAYS.\n\n * the mesh is added once with `panel = -1`, which means EVERY panel -- one upload\n   of the buffer rather than the same buffer sent four times;\n * every panel frames the SAME bounds, so the shape does not appear to breathe as\n   it rotates.\n\nOnly the Kotlin side knows which of the two shapes it is building, which is why\nThreePanel.bounds is computed here and not in the viewer.\n*/\n\nval twisted = marchingCubes(sdfBox(vec(1.2, 1.8, 0.4)).twisted(0.35), boundsAround(radius = 1.2), 80)\nprintln(twisted.summary())\n\nthreeTurntable(twisted, channel = \"turntable\", height = 290)\n\n/*\nFour viewpoints is usually the difference between \"that looks plausible\" and \"yes,\nthat is the shape\". This solid is a good advertisement: at yaw 0 and 180 it is a\nnarrow crescent, at 90 and 270 a broad S.\n\nThings to try:\n  threeTurntable(twisted, views = MeshView.turntable(6, pitchDegrees = 40.0), height = 240)\n  threeTurntable(twisted, wireframe = false)\n*/\n"} "nested" #{}}} {"id" "three-output-renderers" "properties" {"children" #{} "parents" #{#afield/box "root"} "name" "4 — the same mesh, both renderers" "frame" #afield/rect [60.0 480.0 480.0 100.0] "code" #afield/file-inline {"path" "three-output-renderers_code_.kt" "inline" "/*\nWHICH RENDERER DREW THIS, AND WHY THE CPU ONE IS STILL HERE.\n\n`showMesh` defaults to MeshRenderer.GPU now -- the three.js viewer, orbitable. The\nCPU z-buffer rasterizer in VisionGeometryPreview is not deprecated:\n\n  * it is DETERMINISTIC, which is why six test classes assert on its pixels\n    (MeshDefectRenderTest, SolidSLRenderProofTest, MeshIoTest, MeshFromDiskTest,\n    AssimpSceneBridgeTest, MeshVisualizerPixelTest). A GPU capture cannot be an\n    oracle;\n  * it needs no WebGL context, so it draws the inline value-visualizer card that\n    appears when a box ends on a TriMesh -- an iframe per mesh in a list of values\n    is a bad trade, and it is the same ~16-context ceiling ThreePanel dodges;\n  * it renders into a PIXEL BUFFER (TriMesh.renderPixels), for compositing by hand.\n\nRun this and compare the two output channels.\n*/\n\nval solid = marchingCubes(\n    sdfSmoothUnion(sdfSphere(0.78), sdfBox(vec(2.1, 0.32, 0.32)), k = 0.28),\n    boundsAround(radius = 1.35),\n    64,\n)\nval view = MeshView(width = 520, height = 400, wireframe = true)\n\n// NOTE `height = view.height`. `showMesh`'s own `height` (default 430) sizes the\n// IFRAME; MeshView.height sizes the RASTER. Without this the two frames differ.\nshowMesh(\n    solid,\n    channel = \"three.js - the default\",\n    view = view,\n    height = view.height,\n    renderer = MeshRenderer.GPU,\n    title = \"MeshRenderer.GPU - MeshStandardMaterial, orbitable\",\n)\nshowMesh(\n    solid,\n    channel = \"the CPU rasterizer\",\n    view = view,\n    renderer = MeshRenderer.CPU,\n    title = \"MeshRenderer.CPU - two-light lambert, deterministic, still\",\n)\n\n/*\nThe shading differs and is not meant to match. The INPUT is held identical:\ntoThreeDrawable mirrors toCapturedMesh step for step -- the same 35-degree crease\nangle for missing normals, the same DEFAULT_SURFACE grey, back-face culling on so\na wrongly-wound face is MISSING in both. The fact line under each is the same\nsentence, from one implementation.\n\nThe framing is NOT identical, and that is deliberate: the viewer uses the camera\npreset for DIRECTION only and then zoom-to-fits the frame it was actually handed,\nso MeshView.distance becomes a padding factor, max(1.02, distance / 2.7 * 1.06).\nTry distance = 1.0 and distance = 4.0 on the GPU half and watch only the second\none do anything.\n*/\n"} "nested" #{}}} {"id" "three-output-winding" "properties" {"children" #{} "parents" #{#afield/box "root"} "name" "5 — the winding diagnostic, in both renderers" "frame" #afield/rect [60.0 620.0 480.0 100.0] "code" #afield/file-inline {"path" "three-output-winding_code_.kt" "inline" "/*\nTHE DIAGNOSTIC THAT HAD TO SURVIVE THE SWITCH.\n\nA face wound the wrong way is MISSING under back-face culling -- not dark, gone. The polytope and\ntypography docs pages both teach that as a winding test, so the move to the viewer was not allowed\nto switch it off. `ThreeDrawStyle.surface(cullBackFaces = true)` is the default; MeshView's\ncullBackFaces is the rasterizer's. Run this and look at both channels.\n\nReverse the winding of one band of triangles. Not one vertex moves.\n*/\nval ball = icosphereMesh(3, 0.8)\nval idx = ball.indices.copyOf()\nvar reversed = 0\nfor (t in 0 until ball.triangleCount) {\n    val cy = (ball.position(ball.ia(t)).y + ball.position(ball.ib(t)).y + ball.position(ball.ic(t)).y) / 3.0\n    if (cy > 0.30) {\n        val b = idx[t * 3 + 1]\n        idx[t * 3 + 1] = idx[t * 3 + 2]\n        idx[t * 3 + 2] = b\n        reversed++\n    }\n}\nval holed = TriMesh(ball.positions.copyOf(), idx)\n\nprintln(\"reversed %d of %d triangles; not one vertex moved\".format(reversed, ball.triangleCount))\nprintln(\"volume  %.4f  ->  %.4f\".format(ball.signedVolume(), holed.signedVolume()))\nprintln(\"consistently oriented: \" + holed.topology().isConsistentlyOriented)\n\nval view = MeshView(width = 460, height = 380, yawDegrees = 18.0, pitchDegrees = 38.0, wireframe = true)\nshowMesh(\n    holed,\n    channel = \"three.js\",\n    view = view,\n    height = view.height,\n    renderer = MeshRenderer.GPU,\n    title = \"the band is GONE, not dark\",\n)\nshowMesh(\n    holed,\n    channel = \"the CPU rasterizer\",\n    view = view,\n    renderer = MeshRenderer.CPU,\n    title = \"...and gone here too, which is the whole point\",\n)\n\n/*\nORBIT the three.js panel: you are looking through the shell at the inside of its far side.\n\nThen read the numbers against the picture. signedVolume is quietly, catastrophically wrong. The\nfact line still says chi=2 genus 0 watertight, because chi counts vertices, edges and faces and\nreversing a triangle changes none of them -- topology is not orientation. Only\ntopology().isConsistentlyOriented says so, and fixWinding() is the repair.\n\nThings to try:\n  showMesh(holed.fixWinding(), ...)          // the hole closes and the volume comes back\n  MeshView(..., cullBackFaces = false)       // the band returns, shaded inside-out\n  the three.js wireframe is NOT culled, so the missing triangles' edges are still drawn\n*/\n"} "nested" #{}}}] "persistentProperties" ["frame" "name" "code" "code_language" "contents_language" "default_property" "file_proxy_mode" "file_proxy_path" "file_proxy_original_path" "file_proxy_mime" "filename" "preamble" "introduction" "attic" "notes" "geometryReviews" "fragment" "stackSL" "msl" "vertex" "vertex_fragment" "task" "mesh" "raygen" "miss" "closesthit" "anyhit" "intersection" "callable" "fragment_s" "vertex_s" "task_s" "mesh_s" "raygen_s" "miss_s" "closesthit_s" "anyhit_s" "intersection_s" "callable_s" "children" "parents" "compute" "compute_s" "customization" "source_id" "templateId" "_tombstone" "isRemoteJSRoot" "isPythonRoot" ".*_editorState" "metadata" "nested" "nestedIn" "playlist" "pauseList" "bookmarks" "slideShow" "autorun" "layers" "layerFrames" "_controlGroupValues" "ai4_auto_name" "ai4_auto_name_signature" "fileManagerAccessHistory" "isClojureRoot" "isDatalogRoot" "isForthRoot" "isFieldMLRoot" "isMorphoRoot" "isNanoRoot" "nanoLaunchDelayMs" "nanoMemoryInit" "isKotlinJsRoot" "mainLanguage" "spreadsheet"]}